<section class="cp-topic" id="timing">
<h2>When is the time to act?</h2>
<p>The timing table gives, for each county, the year a sea level rise increment (2, 4, 6, 8 or 10 feet, the increments NOAA maps) is reached under each of the three scenarios California's guidance recommends evaluating: Intermediate, Intermediate-high and High.</p>
<p>The years come from Appendix F of the 2024 California Sea Level Rise Guidance, which projects sea level rise in feet above 2000 for {{ opcGaugeProjections.gauges | length }} of the state's tide gauges in each decade from 2030 to 2150, with local vertical land motion included. Between decades the table interpolates linearly, starting from zero in 2000, and rounds to the year. An increment not reached by 2150 is shown as "after 2150". It uses the median projection, so it says when an increment is reached in the middle of the range, not when it could be reached.</p>
<p>The method was checked against NOAA's own table for San Francisco County, which stops at 2100: every year NOAA shows matches, except one that is a year off. The build also asserts the San Francisco table, so a change to the method that moves it fails the build.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="gauges">
<h2>Which tide gauge each county uses</h2>
<p>Sea level rise is not uniform along California's coast, so there is no statewide conversion. Each county takes one reference gauge, an editorial call following the guidance's own regional grouping where it and proximity agree.</p>
<dl class="cp-reasons">
{%- for g in countyProfiles.gaugeGroups %}
<div><dt>{{ g.name }}</dt><dd>{{ g.counties | join(", ") }}</dd></div>
{%- endfor %}
</dl>
<p>Some counties have a second sea level regime: an outer coast and a bay shore. Whether a county <em>straddles</em> is never authored. It is computed at build from the timing tables of the two gauges: a county straddles when any year differs by five or more, or when one gauge reaches an increment by 2150 and the other does not. A straddling county shows both tables.{% for c in countyProfiles.counties %}{% if c.straddle %} {{ c.name }} is currently the only one.{% endif %}{% endfor %}</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="connected-low-lying">
<h2>Connected and low-lying sea level rise exposure</h2>
<p>NOAA's sea level rise mapping separates two kinds of area at each increment: ocean-connected inundation, and isolated low-lying areas that are below the projected water level but that its elevation model does not connect to the ocean (<a href="https://coast.noaa.gov/data/digitalcoast/pdf/slr-inundation-methods.pdf" rel="noopener">NOAA Office for Coastal Management, method description for the Sea Level Rise Viewer, January 2017</a>). County Profiles reports the two combined: each bar or column is the total, with the ocean-connected part solid and the additional low-lying part dashed.</p>
<p>Low-lying areas are included because they can still flood. Drainage the elevation model cannot see may connect them to the water, and coastal groundwater rises with the sea, which can flood areas that have no surface connection to the ocean (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41558-020-0874-1" rel="noopener">Befus, Barnard and Hoover, 2020, <i>Nature Climate Change</i></a>). The two sources are cited separately because they support different points: NOAA's document defines the two kinds of area and warns that the model does not resolve drains and ditches, so unconnected areas may be mapped incorrectly, but it does not discuss groundwater, which rests on Befus et al. The low-lying counts are therefore less certain than the ocean-connected ones. The same combined series is used for jobs under six feet of sea level rise in the total economy topic.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="withheld">
<h2>Withheld values, zeros and gaps</h2>
<p>Three different things can stand where a number would be, and the pages never blur them. <strong>Zero</strong> is an answer: a county with no medical facilities in its floodplain shows 0, and a marine sector with no jobs in a county shows none. <strong>Withheld</strong> is a figure the publisher will not release: ENOW hides values it cannot show without identifying an employer (it writes them as "SUP"), and they appear as "withheld". A total that has a withheld component is marked incomplete with an asterisk, and so is anything derived from it. <strong>Unavailable</strong> is a section or topic with no data at all, and it states its reason where the section would be.</p>
<p>A sector with no jobs has no average wage, so the wages chart leaves it out and says so in its footnote; that is a zero, not a withheld value.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="economy-years">
<h2>Economy data years</h2>
<p>Marine economy figures come from NOAA's Economics: National Ocean Watch (ENOW), whose county-level series ends in 2021: the original dataset is paused, and its successor, Open ENOW, reports states and the nation but not counties. The total economy comes from NOAA's Total Economy (Coastal) series (2023) with self-employed workers from the Census Bureau's Nonemployer Statistics (2023). The marine share of a county's jobs divides 2021 marine jobs by the same county's 2021 total jobs, so the two years match. Each figure's source and year is on its own About this data slide.</p>
<p>The delta counties (Sacramento, San Joaquin and Yolo) are on ENOW's list but are not in the shore-adjacent total economy series, so they have a marine economy and no total economy. Lake, San Benito, Sutter and Trinity are in neither.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="land-cover">
<h2>Land cover</h2>
<p>The natural-features sections use NOAA's C-CAP regional land cover at 30 metres, the 1996 and 2016 epochs (the newest the regional product offers). Each pixel counts once, by its 2016 class. Wetland is the palustrine and estuarine wetland and aquatic-bed classes; upland is grassland, forest, scrub and shrub; other is developed, cultivated, pasture, bare and shore land. Open water is not counted as land. Development added is developed in 2016 and not in 1996. Inside and outside the floodplain, and inside each sea level rise extent, are pixel counts against the same hazard polygons as every other section.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="marine-jobs">
<h2>Marine jobs at risk are not published</h2>
<p>NOAA's original marine economy snapshot reported how many marine businesses were in the floodplain. County Profiles does not report marine jobs at risk, and there is no substitute. Business locations, which made the original figure possible, are licensed data and cannot be reused, and the open replacement, LEHD LODES job counts, is published by 2-digit industry code, while ENOW's marine sectors are defined at up to 6 digits. No open source isolates marine jobs by location.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="methodology-changelog">
<h2>Methodology changelog</h2>
<p>Each profile shows its methodology version ("Version <em>N</em>") on its About this data slide. The version is a monotonic integer, not a date: it increments only when a pipeline change could move a figure while the underlying source data stays the same (switching the intersect method, changing the rollup geography, fixing an aggregation bug), never for a new section, a restyle or an added county. Two snapshots carrying different versions are never joined by a trend line.</p>
<dl class="cp-reasons">
<div><dt>Version 1</dt><dd>Orange County, computed from real data for the first time (Phase 2): block-level areal apportionment of people and jobs to the flood and sea level rise extents.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Version 2</dt><dd>All 27 counties (Phase 3). A county's flood zones are now found through FEMA's political-jurisdiction layer rather than by assuming the study id is the county code (San Francisco and Sutter are numbered by community); sea level rise polygons are read from every NOAA regional file that touches the county, with overlaps between files merged; each county's areas are measured on a projection centred on that county. Orange County was recomputed and its figures may differ slightly from version 1.</dd></div>
</dl>
</section>

